        "category": "engine",
        "title": "Timing belt is an age/interval wear item that can strand the car",
        "description": "All of these Escort four-cylinders (1.9 CVH, 2.0 SPI, 2.0 Zetec) are timing-belt engines, and on cars now decades old the belt is a top no-start/breakdown cause when it has been neglected. Published Ford intervals ran roughly 100k-120k miles depending on year/belt material. Notably all three of these engines are actually non-interference designs (the 2.0 Zetec is widely but incorrectly listed as interference in many shop manuals), so a snapped belt normally strands the car without bending valves. Either way a belt failure leaves the car dead on the road and is a routine replacement these engines require.",
        "solution": "Replace the timing belt on schedule (and any belt of unknown age on a used purchase), ideally together with the tensioner and water pump since the pump is driven off the same area. Use the correct Zetec cam-alignment tooling when doing the DOHC engine so the cams are timed correctly on reassembly.",
        "severity": "medium",
        "confidence": "low",
        "symptoms": [
          "Sudden stall / crank-no-start",
          "Engine cranks but won't fire",
          "Rattle from timing cover before failure",
          "Loss of power at high mileage on an old belt"
        ],
