        "category": "engine",
        "title": "Timing belt failure destroys the 2.0L I4 (interference engine)",
        "description": "The 2.0L FS four-cylinder DOHC is an interference design: if the timing belt snaps or jumps, the pistons strike open valves and bend them, requiring head work. Ford specifies belt replacement at 60,000-mile intervals, but many surviving cars are decades past that and running on original or long-forgotten belts. (The 2.5L KL V6 is generally regarded as non-interference, so a V6 belt failure typically leaves the car stranded but without valve damage, which makes the risk asymmetric between the two engines.) Owners frequently overlook the interval because the car still runs fine right up until the belt lets go.",
        "solution": "Replace the timing belt every 60k miles (or sooner given age-hardened rubber), and do the water pump, tensioner, idler, and cam/crank seals at the same time since they share access. On a 2.0L that has already jumped or snapped the belt, plan for a cylinder-head removal to replace bent valves, or an engine swap. Keep documentation of the last belt service given the cars' age.",
